+# Verified problem generator for arXiv:2307.03794
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph (bipartite perfect matching) |
+| Certificate | integer tuple: an alternative agent-to-house matching |
+| Intuition | invariant: conserved modular sums reveal whole matching layers |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction or discretisation |
+
+## Problem and trust status
+
+The family uses the paper [*Computational complexity of k-stable matchings*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03794) in its native house-allocation model. A solver receives strict incomplete preference lists and a current matching `M`. It must give an alternative matching that every one of the `n` agents strictly prefers, thereby witnessing that `M` is not `n`-stable (equivalently, not weakly Pareto optimal). Checking a response is just an exact range, distinctness, and preference-membership test; `verify` never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+The local construction and verification evidence passes every gate. The mandatory multi-vendor hardness evidence is **not complete**: `harden.py` was run for bare, structural-hint, and placebo prompts, but OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded (total limit)” on every retry. Errors were not counted as model failures. The script-owned error transcripts are retained, but there is no honest `hardened` verdict until the account limit is restored and the runs are repeated.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Section 3.1 defines the matching-market objects, Section 3.2 defines `k`-stability and identifies `n`-stability with weak Pareto optimality, and Section 4.1, Observation 2 identifies improving alternatives with matchings in the improvement graph. Corollary 3 therefore gives the certificate-producing algorithm: Hopcroft–Karp in `O(sqrt(n) m)` time. On eight fixed hard-preset seeds it solved 8/8, using 36,422 edge scans total (roughly 0.02–0.05 seconds total in repeated local runs). This efficient algorithm is why a Track A claim would be false.
+
+Generation samples several cyclic perfect-matching layers, all from the same distribution. In coordinates `i,j` over `Z_n`, every edge in one layer satisfies a common value of `i+j mod n`; agent/house codes and all presentation orders are then shuffled. Once that invariant is noticed, one edge selects a whole layer and the remaining output takes 221 modular additions/subtractions at the hard preset. Without it, the prompt presents a shuffled degree-6 bipartite matching instance. The paper's Theorem 6 gives worst-case NP-completeness for existence at fixed `0<c<1`, but this module does **not** use that theorem as an average-case hardness claim.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=5, layer_count=2, scale=3, seed=0)`, the rendered core is:
+
+```text
+q = 15
+better houses: B0[z=13] B4[z=1] B1[z=4] B2[z=10] B3[z=7]
+A0[z=10]: B2 > B0 > C0 [current]
+A1[z=1]:  B3 > B1 > C1 [current]
+A2[z=7]:  B0 > B4 > C2 [current]
+A3[z=4]:  B1 > B4 > C3 [current]
+A4[z=13]: B3 > B2 > C4 [current]
+```
+
+The answer `[0, 3, 4, 1, 2]` gives each agent a distinct preferred house, so `verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")`. Replacing its last entry by `0` gives `(False, "matching constraint is violated by a repeated house")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by selecting one of the two constant-sum layers.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | n | Layers/row | Scalar scale | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 5 | 2 | 3 | hand-scale illustration |
+| easy | 80 | 4 | 101 | oracle run blocked by HTTP 403 |
+| medium | 150 | 5 | 1,009 | not reached |
+| hard | 220 | 6 | 4,001 | provisional shipping preset; local gates pass |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is `hard`. This is intentionally marked provisional because no oracle level received a scored attempt.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verify |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | 220 entries round-trip through realistic tagged prose; JSON-native |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 valid structure-aware uniform permutation guesses |
+| G5 | shipping density 0/200,000; demo has exactly 2/120 witnesses; shipping Hopcroft–Karp cost is recorded numerically |
+| G6 | 7 attacks × 8 seeds, 0 successes; Hopcroft–Karp and the disclosed compact route both solve 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled `n=440` instance builds and verifies |
+| G8 | 100 relabelling/composition checks pass; 20/20 unrelated affine-canonical keys differ |
+| G9(c) | 771 characters, 193 estimated tokens, 220 atoms, 221 exact operations — all within caps |
+
+Exact wall-clock values from the final machine run are in `selftest_report.json`.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+| Run | Preset reached | Scored solved/attempts | Script result |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 | four retries errored; no verdict |
+| structural hint | easy | 0/0 | four retries errored; no verdict |
+| placebo hint | easy | 0/0 | four retries errored; no verdict |
+
+The structural hint says only that improving edges form conserved-sum layers; it does not give a sequence of steps. Because neither hinted nor placebo obtained a completed call, `hinted − placebo` is undefined, and no conclusion about the intuition effect is possible. The 0/0 counts must not be read as oracle failures.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2307_03794 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
+answer = inst["answer"]                 # a held construction certificate
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+assert g.parse_answer("<answer>" + ",".join(map(str, answer)) + "</answer>") == answer
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit records with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2307.03794
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is a Track B benchmark: any implementation of bipartite matching makes it easy, and the compact conserved-sum recovery also makes it easy once recognized. The zero-hit guess result concerns a uniform prior over all house-code permutations; it does not prove an average-case hardness theorem or tightly estimate the true solution density. The seven failed attacks cover local extrema, list position, nearest scalar, a constant-difference ansatz, deterministic greedy, and 256 randomized greedy restarts; SAT/ILP solvers were not tested because Hopcroft–Karp strictly targets the native problem and already succeeds. The canonical key removes code permutations, orderings, translations, and simultaneous multiplication by units in `Z_n`, but exotic isomorphisms between circulant layer sets may still evade it. Most importantly, the four-vendor no-tool claim remains unverified until the OpenRouter limit is fixed and all three script-owned runs complete.
